Please don't confuse language with writing system. The latter is a
derivation based on culture and tradition. I think few will doubt that
the latin alphabet (+ spelling rules) is much easier to learn than the
japanese system.

Language per se is in part also based on tradition, of course. But
some people have shown that tradition just comes to fill the "holes",
like exact word forms and a system of concretizing of grammatical
markings that make utterances expressive enough (like marking some of
time, gender, subject/object, etc. through morphology, word order,
intonation, etc.--there are different systems). (For a formal
discussion, see Chomsky's _The Minimalist Program_, for an
introduction--Pinker's book or pretty much any introductory
linguistics class or book).

